window.SX_DECK.cards.push(...[{"id":"SX-1121","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-AE06B70089","domain":"Security Operations","objective":"4.4","subdomain":"Incident-response data and artifacts","topic":"image metadata","card_type":"blueprint_exact","front":"CAS-005 blueprint — image metadata: what must you know and apply?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Image metadata can include EXIF/XMP fields such as timestamps, camera/device information, dimensions, software, orientation, and sometimes geolocation. Use it to support provenance and timeline analysis while recognizing metadata can be stripped, altered, or inconsistent with trusted system timestamps."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"For incident-response artifacts, preserve evidence and provenance, prioritize volatile data when appropriate, correlate host/network/cloud context, and distinguish family/behavior evidence from unsupported actor attribution. For image metadata, anchor that reasoning to the direct answer above and require scenario evidence before selecting or rejecting it."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"<b>Case.</b> Incident responders are analyzing a live compromise and must preserve evidence, understand malware behavior, and recover safely. The scenario turns on this condition: Image metadata can include EXIF/XMP fields such as timestamps, camera/device information, dimensions, software, orientation, and sometimes geolocation. Use it to support provenance and timeline analysis while recognizing metadata can be stripped, altered, or inconsistent with trusted system timestamps.<br><br><b>Decision.</b> Select or apply <b>image metadata</b>. Image metadata can include EXIF/XMP fields such as timestamps, camera/device information, dimensions, software, orientation, and sometimes geolocation. Use it to support provenance and timeline analysis while recognizing metadata can be stripped, altered, or inconsistent with trusted system timestamps.<br><br><b>Reasoning.</b> Image metadata can include EXIF/XMP fields such as timestamps, camera/device information, dimensions, software, orientation, and sometimes geolocation. Use it to support provenance and timeline analysis while recognizing metadata can be stripped, altered, or inconsistent with trusted system timestamps.<br><br><b>Discriminator.</b> The discriminator is scope: use image metadata only when its stated property solves the requirement; a stronger-sounding control at another layer is not automatically the better answer."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Do not destroy volatile evidence prematurely or overstate attribution from similarity alone; preserve originals, normalize time, document provenance, and corroborate findings. For image metadata, keep the direct-answer scope explicit and reject alternatives that solve a different layer or failure mode."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>image metadata</b> with Malware detonation, Volatile-memory analysis, Network forensics, Timeline reconstruction. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Image metadata can include EXIF/XMP fields such as timestamps, camera/device information, dimensions, software, orientation, and sometimes geolocation."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"In a novel scenario, what evidence would make image metadata relevant, what would you do with that information, and what nearby concept would you reject?"},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master image metadata when you can: (1) explain it without notes, (2) recognize it in a new architecture/log/incident scenario, (3) apply or troubleshoot it correctly, (4) contrast the nearest confusable concept, and (5) state one limitation, tradeoff, or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-FORENSICS: NIST SP 800-86 — Guide to Integrating Forensic Techniques into Incident Response — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/86/final"}],"difficulty":2,"stage":"Foundation","estimated_seconds":60,"prerequisites":["SX-0499"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-FORENSICS"],"tags":["audit_v3","blueprint_exact","objective_4.4"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master image metadata when you can: (1) explain it without notes, (2) recognize it in a new architecture/log/incident scenario, (3) apply or troubleshoot it correctly, (4) contrast the nearest confusable concept, and (5) state one limitation, tradeoff, or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-4.4-017","objective":"4.4","topic":"image metadata"}]},{"id":"SX-1122","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-5C02986501","domain":"Security Operations","objective":"4.4","subdomain":"Incident-response data and artifacts","topic":"file filesystem metadata","card_type":"blueprint_exact","front":"CAS-005 blueprint — file filesystem metadata: what must you know and apply?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"File/filesystem metadata includes names, paths, sizes, ownership, permissions, hashes, timestamps, alternate data/extended attributes, and filesystem records. Correlate it with other evidence because timestamps and attributes can be modified or affected by copying, mounting, and clock differences."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"For incident-response artifacts, preserve evidence and provenance, prioritize volatile data when appropriate, correlate host/network/cloud context, and distinguish family/behavior evidence from unsupported actor attribution. For file filesystem metadata, anchor that reasoning to the direct answer above and require scenario evidence before selecting or rejecting it."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"<b>Case.</b> Incident responders are analyzing a live compromise and must preserve evidence, understand malware behavior, and recover safely. The scenario turns on this condition: File/filesystem metadata includes names, paths, sizes, ownership, permissions, hashes, timestamps, alternate data/extended attributes, and filesystem records. Correlate it with other evidence because timestamps and attributes can be modified or affected by copying, mounting, and clock differences.<br><br><b>Decision.</b> Select or apply <b>file filesystem metadata</b>. File/filesystem metadata includes names, paths, sizes, ownership, permissions, hashes, timestamps, alternate data/extended attributes, and filesystem records. Correlate it with other evidence because timestamps and attributes can be modified or affected by copying, mounting, and clock differences.<br><br><b>Reasoning.</b> File/filesystem metadata includes names, paths, sizes, ownership, permissions, hashes, timestamps, alternate data/extended attributes, and filesystem records. Correlate it with other evidence because timestamps and attributes can be modified or affected by copying, mounting, and clock differences.<br><br><b>Discriminator.</b> The discriminator is scope: use file filesystem metadata only when its stated property solves the requirement; a stronger-sounding control at another layer is not automatically the better answer."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Do not destroy volatile evidence prematurely or overstate attribution from similarity alone; preserve originals, normalize time, document provenance, and corroborate findings. For file filesystem metadata, keep the direct-answer scope explicit and reject alternatives that solve a different layer or failure mode."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>file filesystem metadata</b> with Malware detonation, Volatile-memory analysis, Network forensics, Timeline reconstruction. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> File/filesystem metadata includes names, paths, sizes, ownership, permissions, hashes, timestamps, alternate data/extended attributes, and filesystem records."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"In a novel scenario, what evidence would make file filesystem metadata relevant, what would you do with that information, and what nearby concept would you reject?"},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master file filesystem metadata when you can: (1) explain it without notes, (2) recognize it in a new architecture/log/incident scenario, (3) apply or troubleshoot it correctly, (4) contrast the nearest confusable concept, and (5) state one limitation, tradeoff, or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-FORENSICS: NIST SP 800-86 — Guide to Integrating Forensic Techniques into Incident Response — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/86/final"}],"difficulty":2,"stage":"Foundation","estimated_seconds":60,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-FORENSICS"],"tags":["audit_v3","blueprint_exact","objective_4.4"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master file filesystem metadata when you can: (1) explain it without notes, (2) recognize it in a new architecture/log/incident scenario, (3) apply or troubleshoot it correctly, (4) contrast the nearest confusable concept, and (5) state one limitation, tradeoff, or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-4.4-019","objective":"4.4","topic":"file filesystem metadata"}]},{"id":"SX-1123","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-0201A9D3E6","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.1","subdomain":"IAM troubleshooting","topic":"OpenID in CAS-005 IAM context","card_type":"correction_v3","front":"What is OpenID in the CAS-005 IAM context, and how does it relate to OpenID Connect?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"OpenID is a family of decentralized identity/authentication specifications. Modern application scenarios usually use OpenID Connect (OIDC), an identity layer built on OAuth 2.0; OAuth alone should not be treated as user authentication."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"For CAS-005, distinguish the older OpenID family/name from OpenID Connect. OIDC standardizes authentication and identity claims using OAuth 2.0 flows and ID tokens, while OAuth 2.0 by itself delegates authorization to protected resources."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A web application receives an OAuth access token and assumes it proves who the user is. Use an OIDC authentication flow and validate the ID token's issuer, audience, signature, time claims, and nonce/state protections rather than using the access token as an identity assertion."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Do not equate OAuth with authentication. Also do not assume every historical use of the word OpenID means OIDC; the current application-focused identity layer is OpenID Connect."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>OpenID in CAS-005 IAM context</b> with PAM, SAML, OAuth, OpenID Connect. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> OpenID is a family of decentralized identity/authentication specifications."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given an OAuth/OIDC login flow, identify which token proves user authentication to the client and which token authorizes API access."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master OpenID/OIDC when you can explain the OAuth-versus-OIDC distinction without notes, validate the critical ID-token claims in a scenario, and identify why an access token alone is not a user-authentication assertion."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-OIDC: OpenID Connect Core 1.0 — https://openid.net/specs/openid-connect-core-1_0.html"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":90,"prerequisites":["SX-0247"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-OIDC"],"tags":["audit_v3","correction_v3","objective_3.1","oidc","openid"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master OpenID/OIDC when you can explain the OAuth-versus-OIDC distinction without notes, validate the critical ID-token claims in a scenario, and identify why an access token alone is not a user-authentication assertion.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.1-021","objective":"3.1","topic":"OpenID"}]},{"id":"SX-1124","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-5CBA14E457","domain":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","objective":"1.1","subdomain":"Governance components","topic":"Governance reporting","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What makes security-program reporting an effective governance component?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Governance reporting communicates security posture, risks, incidents, control performance, exceptions, and decisions to the appropriate stakeholders at an appropriate cadence."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Reports should be audience-specific, traceable to reliable evidence, and tied to decision ownership; executive reporting emphasizes business risk while operational reporting preserves actionable technical detail."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A security team produces hundreds of technical alerts but leadership cannot tell which risks require funding or acceptance. Build a report that summarizes business impact, trend, control status, exceptions, and the named decision owner."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Reporting is not the same as raw logging. Logs are evidence; governance reporting selects, contextualizes, and communicates information needed for oversight and decisions."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Governance reporting</b> with Security metric, Compliance tracking, Management commitment. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Governance reporting communicates security posture, risks, incidents, control performance, exceptions, and decisions to the appropriate stakeholders at an appropriate cadence."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given a mixed set of technical findings, decide what belongs in an executive governance report versus an analyst report and justify the difference."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Governance reporting when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_1.1","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Governance reporting when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-1.1-013","objective":"1.1","topic":"reporting"}]},{"id":"SX-1125","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-003585AE88","domain":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","objective":"1.1","subdomain":"Governance components","topic":"Asset inventory","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What is the SecurityX purpose of an asset inventory within governance and configuration management?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"An asset inventory is an authoritative record of relevant hardware, software, services, identities, data stores, and ownership needed to manage security throughout their life cycles."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"An inventory should identify what exists, who owns it, where it is, its criticality/classification, and enough configuration or relationship data to support patching, monitoring, incident response, and change management."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A critical library vulnerability is announced, but the company cannot identify which servers and applications use the affected software. The immediate governance weakness is incomplete asset/software inventory."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A CMDB can contain configuration items and relationships, but 'inventory' is the broader requirement to know what assets exist; the two often complement one another."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Asset inventory</b> with Asset lifecycle management, CMDB, Asset identification and management in Zero Trust. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> An asset inventory is an authoritative record of relevant hardware, software, services, identities, data stores, and ownership needed to manage security throughout their life cy..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Design the minimum inventory fields required to prioritize remediation for an Internet-facing application and explain why each field matters."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Asset inventory when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0017"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_1.1","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Asset inventory when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-1.1-020","objective":"1.1","topic":"inventory"}]},{"id":"SX-1126","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-419B209B53","domain":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","objective":"1.1","subdomain":"Governance components","topic":"Production and non-production data governance","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How should data governance differ across production, development, testing, QA, and staging environments?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Apply environment-appropriate access, masking or synthetic data, retention, monitoring, and promotion controls so sensitive production data is not copied into lower-trust environments without a justified need and equivalent protection."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Production is normally the authoritative live environment; development, test, QA, and staging should receive only the data necessary for their purpose, with controlled movement between environments and explicit lifecycle rules."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Developers copy a full production customer database into QA to reproduce a defect. Replace the copy with masked or synthetic test data where possible, restrict access, define retention, and control any approved production-data transfer."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Calling an environment 'internal' or 'non-production' does not make sensitive data safe. Lower environments often have broader access and weaker monitoring."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Production and non-production data governance</b> with Policy, Standard, RACI, GRC platform. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Apply environment-appropriate access, masking or synthetic data, retention, monitoring, and promotion controls so sensitive production data is not copied into lower-trust enviro..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given five datasets needed for development and testing, decide which may be copied, masked, synthesized, or prohibited and explain the governance basis."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Production and non-production data governance when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0021"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_1.1","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Production and non-production data governance when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-1.1-028","objective":"1.1","topic":"production"},{"id":"BP-1.1-029","objective":"1.1","topic":"development"}]},{"id":"SX-1127","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-6F5D48C2E1","domain":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","objective":"1.2","subdomain":"Risk management","topic":"Risk validation","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What does validation mean after risk remediation?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Risk validation verifies that a remediation or control was implemented as intended, actually reduced the targeted risk, and did not leave the organization relying on an untested assumption."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Validation may include retesting, control testing, evidence review, rescanning, adversarial testing, or measurement against defined acceptance criteria; remaining exposure becomes residual risk."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A team patches a critical Internet-facing vulnerability and closes the ticket without retesting. The missing step is validation: confirm the vulnerable condition is gone and the control path works."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Implementation evidence alone is not proof of effectiveness. A ticket marked complete can still leave the original exposure present."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Risk validation</b> with Quantitative risk analysis, Risk appetite, RTO, RPO. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Risk validation verifies that a remediation or control was implemented as intended, actually reduced the targeted risk, and did not leave the organization relying on an untested..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"After a compensating control is deployed for an unpatchable system, define evidence that would validate risk reduction."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Risk validation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-RISK: NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 Guide for Conducting Risk Assessments — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/30/r1/final"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0027"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-RISK"],"tags":["objective_1.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Risk validation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-1.2-011","objective":"1.2","topic":"validation"}]},{"id":"SX-1128","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-4BCC0CA64C","domain":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","objective":"1.2","subdomain":"Risk management","topic":"Business continuity and disaster recovery testing","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"Why must business-continuity and disaster-recovery plans be tested?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"BC/DR testing verifies that people, procedures, dependencies, backups, communications, and recovery technology can meet required continuity and recovery objectives under realistic disruption."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Exercises can range from tabletop walkthroughs to partial or full failover tests; findings should update plans, dependencies, RTO/RPO assumptions, and remediation priorities."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A company has replicated databases and documented failover procedures but has never executed them. A controlled failover test reveals DNS and identity dependencies that make the stated RTO impossible."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Having backups or a written plan does not prove recoverability. Tests must consider dependencies and should be designed so the exercise itself does not create unacceptable production risk."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Business continuity and disaster recovery testing</b> with Business continuity, Disaster recovery, RTO, RPO. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> BC/DR testing verifies that people, procedures, dependencies, backups, communications, and recovery technology can meet required continuity and recovery objectives under realist..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Choose an appropriate test type for a life-safety system versus a noncritical SaaS workload and explain the tradeoff."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Business continuity and disaster recovery testing when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-RISK: NIST SP 800-30 Rev. 1 Guide for Conducting Risk Assessments — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/30/r1/final"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0037"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-RISK"],"tags":["objective_1.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Business continuity and disaster recovery testing when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-1.2-018","objective":"1.2","topic":"testing"}]},{"id":"SX-1129","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-BB979F55E2","domain":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","objective":"1.4","subdomain":"Threat modeling","topic":"Attack pattern","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What is an attack pattern in threat modeling?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"An attack pattern is a reusable description of how an adversary can exploit a recurring weakness or interaction to achieve an objective."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Attack patterns help model likely attacker methods independently of one specific incident; catalogs such as CAPEC can organize patterns, while ATT&CK focuses on observed adversary tactics and techniques."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Multiple applications expose the same unsafe trust relationship. Instead of documenting each exploit as unrelated, model the recurring adversary method as an attack pattern and select controls that break the pattern."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"An attack pattern is not an indicator of compromise and is not identical to an ATT&CK technique; it describes a reusable attack approach."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Attack pattern</b> with MITRE ATT&CK, STRIDE, Trust boundary, Attack tree. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> An attack pattern is a reusable description of how an adversary can exploit a recurring weakness or interaction to achieve an objective."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given a new application design, identify one reusable attack pattern and show how two different controls could interrupt it."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Attack pattern when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-MITRE-CAPEC: MITRE CAPEC — https://capec.mitre.org/"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0085"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-MITRE-CAPEC"],"tags":["objective_1.4","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Attack pattern when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-1.4-012","objective":"1.4","topic":"attack patterns"}]},{"id":"SX-1130","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-707174DEF2","domain":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","objective":"1.5","subdomain":"AI adoption challenges","topic":"DLP for AI assistants","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How should DLP be applied to AI-enabled assistants and digital workers?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"DLP for AI assistants constrains sensitive data entering prompts, context, retrieval, logs, tools, and outputs, while enforcing approved destinations and least-privileged data access."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Controls can include classification-aware access, prompt/input inspection, retrieval filters, output inspection, redaction, destination restrictions, retention limits, and human approval for sensitive transfers."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: An AI support assistant can search customer records and send email. Prevent it from placing regulated data into arbitrary external messages by limiting retrieval scope and applying output/destination DLP before send actions."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"DLP does not make an overprivileged agent safe by itself; combine it with least privilege, guardrails, authorization, and audit logging."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>DLP for AI assistants</b> with Sensitive-information disclosure, Excessive agency, AI assistant access permissions. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> DLP for AI assistants constrains sensitive data entering prompts, context, retrieval, logs, tools, and outputs, while enforcing approved destinations and least-privileged data a..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Design a DLP policy for an AI assistant that can summarize internal documents but must never disclose restricted material externally."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master DLP for AI assistants when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-AIRMF: NIST AI Risk Management Framework — https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-AIRMF"],"tags":["objective_1.5","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master DLP for AI assistants when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-1.5-025","objective":"1.5","topic":"DLP"}]},{"id":"SX-1131","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-D001AE05A6","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.2","subdomain":"Secure systems life cycle","topic":"Hardware supply-chain risk management","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How should hardware supply-chain risk be managed across the system life cycle?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Hardware supply-chain risk management verifies provenance, supplier trust, component authenticity, custody, firmware integrity, lifecycle support, and exposure to counterfeit or maliciously modified components."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Controls can include approved suppliers, component traceability, tamper-evident handling, validation/testing, signed firmware, diversification for critical dependencies, and monitoring for advisories or supplier changes."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A critical appliance arrives from an unapproved reseller at a steep discount. Before deployment, verify provenance and authenticity rather than relying only on a functional power-on test."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Hardware assurance overlaps with supply-chain management but is not identical: supply-chain risk covers the upstream relationships and component path, while assurance evaluates whether the hardware meets required trust properties."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Hardware supply-chain risk management</b> with SAST, DAST, SCA, SBOM. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Hardware supply-chain risk management verifies provenance, supplier trust, component authenticity, custody, firmware integrity, lifecycle support, and exposure to counterfeit or..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"For a high-assurance cryptographic appliance, define three supply-chain controls that should exist before acceptance."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Hardware supply-chain risk management when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0159"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_2.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Hardware supply-chain risk management when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.2-023","objective":"2.2","topic":"hardware supply chain"}]},{"id":"SX-1132","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-5BA44CE38F","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.4","subdomain":"Access/authentication/authorization design","topic":"Federation service provider","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What is a service provider in a federated identity architecture?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"A federation service provider is the relying application or service that consumes an identity provider's assertion or token and grants access according to its local authorization policy."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"In SAML, the service provider typically redirects authentication to an identity provider and validates returned assertions; in other federation patterns the analogous party is often called a relying party or client."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Employees authenticate at a corporate IdP and then access a partner SaaS application. The SaaS application is the service provider because it relies on the IdP's assertion and still enforces its own authorization."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A service provider should not blindly trust every assertion: validate signature, issuer, audience/recipient, conditions, time, and the configured trust relationship."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Federation service provider</b> with Federation, SSO, ABAC, Policy enforcement point. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> A federation service provider is the relying application or service that consumes an identity provider's assertion or token and grants access according to its local authorizatio..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given a three-party federation flow, identify the IdP and SP and list the assertion checks the SP must perform."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Federation service provider when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-OASIS-SAML: OASIS SAML 2.0 — https://www.oasis-open.org/standard/saml/"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0191"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-OASIS-SAML"],"tags":["objective_2.4","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Federation service provider when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.4-009","objective":"2.4","topic":"service provider"}]},{"id":"SX-1133","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-B5E038A2AB","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.4","subdomain":"Access/authentication/authorization design","topic":"Certificate template","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What is the security role of a certificate template in PKI architecture?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"A certificate template defines approved enrollment and certificate properties such as subject information, key usage, extended key usage, validity, key requirements, issuance permissions, and approval behavior."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Templates standardize issuance and can automate safe enrollment, but overly permissive enrollment rights or dangerous subject/EKU settings can create privilege-escalation paths."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Ordinary users can enroll a certificate template that permits client authentication while allowing requester-supplied identities. Tighten template permissions and identity controls before relying on the PKI."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A template is not a certificate authority and does not itself establish trust; the CA issues certificates under template/policy constraints."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Certificate template</b> with PKI architecture, certificate authority, Credential issuance. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> A certificate template defines approved enrollment and certificate properties such as subject information, key usage, extended key usage, validity, key requirements, issuance pe..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Review a hypothetical certificate template and identify one configuration that could allow impersonation."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Certificate template when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-MS-CERT-TEMPLATES: Microsoft Learn — Certificate template concepts in Windows Server — https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-cs/certificate-template-concepts"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-MS-CERT-TEMPLATES"],"tags":["objective_2.4","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Certificate template when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.4-025","objective":"2.4","topic":"certificate templates"}]},{"id":"SX-1134","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-B45DC8840A","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.6","subdomain":"Zero Trust architecture","topic":"Segmentation in Zero Trust","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How does segmentation support Zero Trust architecture?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Segmentation divides networks or environments into controlled zones so policy can restrict communication paths, reduce implicit reachability, and limit lateral movement; Zero Trust then applies identity- and context-based decisions rather than treating a segment as trusted."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Segmentation is coarser than microsegmentation but can still establish useful security boundaries around user, server, management, OT, or cloud zones."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A compromised workstation can reach every database subnet because all internal networks are mutually routable. Introduce segmentation and explicit policy, then apply stronger workload-level controls where needed."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Segmentation alone is not Zero Trust. Being inside an allowed subnet must not replace subject identity, device posture, authorization, and continuous evaluation."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Segmentation in Zero Trust</b> with Zero Trust, Continuous authorization, Microsegmentation, SASE. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Segmentation divides networks or environments into controlled zones so policy can restrict communication paths, reduce implicit reachability, and limit lateral movement; Zero Tr..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Design a segmented network for users, management systems, and sensitive workloads, then state which decisions still require Zero Trust identity/context checks."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Segmentation in Zero Trust when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-ZT: NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0227"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-ZT"],"tags":["objective_2.6","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Segmentation in Zero Trust when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.6-003","objective":"2.6","topic":"segmentation"}]},{"id":"SX-1135","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-671CEA7FE0","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.6","subdomain":"Zero Trust architecture","topic":"VPN in Zero Trust architecture","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What role can a VPN play in a Zero Trust architecture?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"A VPN can provide an authenticated encrypted transport path, but Zero Trust must not treat successful VPN connection as sufficient trust; each resource request still requires appropriate identity, device, context, and authorization checks."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Always-on VPN can ensure traffic traverses managed controls, while application- or resource-level policy limits what the connected subject can actually reach."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A contractor connects successfully to the corporate VPN. Instead of granting broad internal network access, restrict the contractor to the approved application and continuously evaluate identity/device context."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"VPN confidentiality and authentication do not automatically provide least privilege, application authorization, device trust, or segmentation."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>VPN in Zero Trust architecture</b> with Zero Trust, Continuous authorization, Microsegmentation, SASE. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> A VPN can provide an authenticated encrypted transport path, but Zero Trust must not treat successful VPN connection as sufficient trust; each resource request still requires ap..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given a remote-access design, identify what the VPN protects and what additional Zero Trust controls are still required after tunnel establishment."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master VPN in Zero Trust architecture when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-ZT: NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0223"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-ZT"],"tags":["objective_2.6","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master VPN in Zero Trust architecture when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.6-005","objective":"2.6","topic":"VPN"}]},{"id":"SX-1136","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-130B4A41D7","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.6","subdomain":"Zero Trust architecture","topic":"Security-boundary system components","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How should system components be handled when defining Zero Trust security boundaries?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Identify every component that stores, processes, transmits, brokers, or enforces access to protected resources, then place explicit trust boundaries and policy enforcement around the relevant interactions."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Components include clients, services, gateways, workloads, identity systems, data stores, control planes, and supporting infrastructure; trust should be based on verified relationships rather than physical or network proximity."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: An internal message broker sits between two microservices and was omitted from the trust model. Add it as a component because compromise could alter or replay authorized data flows."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A boundary diagram that ignores supporting components can create hidden transitive trust and bypass paths."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Security-boundary system components</b> with Zero Trust, Continuous authorization, Microsegmentation, SASE. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Identify every component that stores, processes, transmits, brokers, or enforces access to protected resources, then place explicit trust boundaries and policy enforcement aroun..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given an application data flow, enumerate the components that must appear in the Zero Trust boundary model and justify each."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Security-boundary system components when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-ZT: NIST SP 800-207 Zero Trust Architecture — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/207/final"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-ZT"],"tags":["objective_2.6","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Security-boundary system components when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.6-011","objective":"2.6","topic":"system components"}]},{"id":"SX-1137","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-5A4D322DEC","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.1","subdomain":"IAM troubleshooting","topic":"SSO troubleshooting","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What should you verify when troubleshooting single sign-on?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Trace the SSO flow end to end: user/session state, identity-provider authentication, federation protocol, token/assertion issuance, signatures, issuer/audience/recipient, time conditions, claims, service-provider trust, and local authorization."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"SSO is a user experience and trust architecture, not one protocol; failures can occur in SAML, OIDC, Kerberos, session/cookie handling, certificate trust, claims mapping, or authorization after successful authentication."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A user authenticates successfully at the IdP but one SaaS application rejects access. Check the application's trust, audience/recipient, assertion/token validity, claims mapping, and local authorization before resetting the user's password."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Do not assume an SSO failure is an authentication failure. The user may authenticate correctly while token validation or authorization fails downstream."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>SSO troubleshooting</b> with PAM, SAML, OAuth, OpenID Connect. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Trace the SSO flow end to end: user/session state, identity-provider authentication, federation protocol, token/assertion issuance, signatures, issuer/audience/recipient, time c..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given an SSO symptom, identify which stage of the flow you would test first and what evidence would confirm the fault."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master SSO troubleshooting when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-OASIS-SAML: OASIS SAML 2.0 — https://www.oasis-open.org/standard/saml/"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-OASIS-SAML"],"tags":["objective_3.1","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master SSO troubleshooting when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.1-023","objective":"3.1","topic":"SSO"}]},{"id":"SX-1138","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-72698CBC47","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.1","subdomain":"IAM troubleshooting","topic":"Federation troubleshooting","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What should you verify when troubleshooting federation?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Verify the configured trust relationship, issuer and relying-party identifiers, signing/encryption certificates, endpoints, metadata, protocol settings, token/assertion audience and time conditions, claims mapping, and authorization at the relying service."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Federation lets separate identity domains trust assertions or tokens; troubleshooting must distinguish identity proof/authentication at the IdP from token validation and authorization at the service provider."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: All users from a partner suddenly fail after the partner rotates its signing certificate. Update and validate federation metadata/trust rather than changing user passwords."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A valid token from an untrusted issuer, wrong audience, expired window, or unknown signing key must still be rejected."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Federation troubleshooting</b> with PAM, SAML, OAuth, OpenID Connect. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Verify the configured trust relationship, issuer and relying-party identifiers, signing/encryption certificates, endpoints, metadata, protocol settings, token/assertion audience..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Diagnose a federation outage caused by either certificate rotation, audience mismatch, or clock skew and state the confirming evidence for each."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Federation troubleshooting when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-OASIS-SAML: OASIS SAML 2.0 — https://www.oasis-open.org/standard/saml/"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0255"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-OASIS-SAML"],"tags":["objective_3.1","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Federation troubleshooting when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.1-031","objective":"3.1","topic":"federation"}]},{"id":"SX-1139","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-A25949481A","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.2","subdomain":"Endpoint and server security","topic":"Anti-malware","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What does anti-malware contribute to endpoint and server security?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Anti-malware detects, blocks, quarantines, or removes malicious software using signatures, reputation, heuristics, behavior, or other analysis, and should be maintained and monitored as one layer of endpoint defense."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Modern endpoint protection may overlap with EDR, but anti-malware focuses on prevention/detection of malicious code while EDR adds richer telemetry, investigation, and response."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Commodity malware is repeatedly downloaded to endpoints. Keep signatures/engines current and use prevention/quarantine, while EDR investigates process behavior and wider compromise."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Anti-malware is not sufficient against every living-off-the-land, credential, or fileless technique; combine it with application control, EDR, least privilege, patching, and hardening."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Anti-malware</b> with Application allowlisting, EDR, Endpoint privilege management, SELinux. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Anti-malware detects, blocks, quarantines, or removes malicious software using signatures, reputation, heuristics, behavior, or other analysis, and should be maintained and moni..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given a fileless attack using a trusted signed utility, explain why anti-malware alone may miss it and what complementary telemetry/control you would use."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Anti-malware when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-MALWARE: NIST SP 800-83 Rev. 1 — Guide to Malware Incident Prevention and Handling — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/83/r1/final; SRC-NIST-OT: NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 — Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final; SRC-CISA-ICS: CISA Industrial Control Systems — https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-MALWARE","SRC-NIST-OT","SRC-CISA-ICS"],"tags":["objective_3.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Anti-malware when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.2-007","objective":"3.2","topic":"anti-malware"}]},{"id":"SX-1140","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-1510D8B69A","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.2","subdomain":"Endpoint and server security","topic":"Endpoint configuration management","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"Why is configuration management a security control for endpoints and servers?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Configuration management defines, deploys, verifies, and restores approved security baselines so systems do not drift into inconsistent or insecure states."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Use managed policy, configuration-as-code, MDM/endpoint tools, compliance checks, and controlled exceptions to maintain settings such as services, firewall rules, permissions, logging, and security features."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Hundreds of servers slowly diverge from the hardened baseline because administrators make local changes. Enforce and continuously verify the desired configuration rather than relying on a one-time build image."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Configuration management is broader than patching: a fully patched system can still be insecure because of permissions, services, logging, or policy drift."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Endpoint configuration management</b> with Configuration drift, Hardening, Application allowlisting. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Configuration management defines, deploys, verifies, and restores approved security baselines so systems do not drift into inconsistent or insecure states."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Design a control loop that detects and repairs a disabled endpoint firewall setting without overwriting an approved exception."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Endpoint configuration management when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-OT: NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 — Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final; SRC-CISA-ICS: CISA Industrial Control Systems — https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0267"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-OT","SRC-CISA-ICS"],"tags":["objective_3.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Endpoint configuration management when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.2-011","objective":"3.2","topic":"configuration management"}]},{"id":"SX-1141","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-6CC10781D8","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.5","subdomain":"Specialized and legacy systems","topic":"OT and specialized-system segmentation","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"Why is segmentation especially important for OT, ICS, IoT, and legacy systems?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Segmentation limits communication between specialized systems and less-trusted networks, reducing attack paths and containing compromise when endpoints cannot support modern host controls."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Use zones and tightly controlled conduits, protocol/port allowlists, jump hosts or gateways, and restricted remote access while preserving required safety and process communication."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Legacy PLCs cannot run endpoint agents and currently share a flat network with user workstations. Isolate the control zone and allow only required management and process flows through monitored gateways."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Do not apply segmentation changes blindly to safety-critical systems; validate process dependencies and fail behavior before enforcing blocks."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>OT and specialized-system segmentation</b> with OT security, ICS/SCADA, OT and specialized-system monitoring, Safety constraints in specialized systems. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Segmentation limits communication between specialized systems and less-trusted networks, reducing attack paths and containing compromise when endpoints cannot support modern hos..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Design a zone-and-conduit model for engineering workstations, PLCs, historians, and corporate IT and justify every permitted cross-zone flow."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master OT and specialized-system segmentation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-OT: NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 — Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final; SRC-CISA-ICS: CISA Industrial Control Systems — https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0331"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-OT","SRC-CISA-ICS"],"tags":["objective_3.5","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master OT and specialized-system segmentation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.5-009","objective":"3.5","topic":"segmentation"}]},{"id":"SX-1142","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-FA71E33969","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.5","subdomain":"Specialized and legacy systems","topic":"OT and specialized-system monitoring","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How should monitoring be adapted for OT, ICS, IoT, and highly constrained systems?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Prefer monitoring that provides useful visibility without disrupting fragile or safety-critical processes, often using passive network telemetry, device logs, process data, and protocol-aware analysis."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Baselines should include both cyber behavior and expected physical/process behavior so analysts can distinguish attacks from legitimate operational changes."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: An old medical device crashes during aggressive active scans. Replace frequent intrusive scanning with passive monitoring and vendor-approved assessment methods while monitoring network and process anomalies."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Monitoring cannot substitute for segmentation or access control, and passive visibility may not reveal every host-local condition."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>OT and specialized-system monitoring</b> with OT security, ICS/SCADA, OT and specialized-system segmentation, Safety constraints in specialized systems. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Prefer monitoring that provides useful visibility without disrupting fragile or safety-critical processes, often using passive network telemetry, device logs, process data, and..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Choose safe monitoring techniques for an unsupported PLC and explain what visibility each provides and misses."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master OT and specialized-system monitoring when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-OT: NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 — Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final; SRC-CISA-ICS: CISA Industrial Control Systems — https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-OT","SRC-CISA-ICS"],"tags":["objective_3.5","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master OT and specialized-system monitoring when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.5-010","objective":"3.5","topic":"monitoring"}]},{"id":"SX-1143","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-0D26373B40","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.5","subdomain":"Specialized and legacy systems","topic":"Regulatory constraints in specialized systems","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How can regulatory requirements change security engineering for specialized and legacy systems?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Sector-specific regulations, safety rules, validation requirements, records obligations, and change-control constraints can limit how and when specialized systems are modified, monitored, tested, or taken offline."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Engineers must satisfy the security objective while preserving mandated operational, evidence, qualification, and safety requirements; compensating controls may be necessary where direct change is restricted."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A regulated production controller requires formal revalidation after firmware changes. Use network isolation and monitoring while planning a controlled, approved upgrade rather than applying an unscheduled patch."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Regulatory constraints do not justify ignoring known risk; document the constraint, apply compensating controls, obtain proper risk decisions, and plan compliant remediation."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Regulatory constraints in specialized systems</b> with OT security, ICS/SCADA, OT and specialized-system segmentation, OT and specialized-system monitoring. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Sector-specific regulations, safety rules, validation requirements, records obligations, and change-control constraints can limit how and when specialized systems are modified,..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given a security fix that would invalidate a certified process, design a compliant interim risk treatment and a path to permanent remediation."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Regulatory constraints in specialized systems when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-OT: NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 — Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final; SRC-CISA-ICS: CISA Industrial Control Systems — https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-OT","SRC-CISA-ICS"],"tags":["objective_3.5","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Regulatory constraints in specialized systems when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.5-015","objective":"3.5","topic":"regulatory"}]},{"id":"SX-1144","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-80D0BF3F24","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.5","subdomain":"Specialized and legacy systems","topic":"Safety constraints in specialized systems","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How should safety affect cybersecurity decisions in OT and other specialized systems?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"When cyber controls can affect physical processes, life safety and process safety are primary constraints: changes, containment, fail behavior, and response must avoid creating a more dangerous physical condition."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Coordinate with operators and safety engineering, understand process dependencies, test changes safely, and use isolation or graceful degradation where abrupt shutdown or blocking could cause harm."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: During an incident, immediately powering off a compromised controller would overpressurize equipment. Isolate malicious communications while maintaining the safe control state and coordinate a controlled shutdown."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"The usual IT preference for rapid containment or fail-secure behavior may be wrong when it creates physical danger."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Safety constraints in specialized systems</b> with OT security, ICS/SCADA, OT and specialized-system segmentation, OT and specialized-system monitoring. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> When cyber controls can affect physical processes, life safety and process safety are primary constraints: changes, containment, fail behavior, and response must avoid creating..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given two containment options for a compromised industrial controller, choose the safer one and explain both cyber and physical consequences."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Safety constraints in specialized systems when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-OT: NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 — Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final; SRC-CISA-ICS: CISA Industrial Control Systems — https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-OT","SRC-CISA-ICS"],"tags":["objective_3.5","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Safety constraints in specialized systems when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.5-016","objective":"3.5","topic":"safety"}]},{"id":"SX-1145","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-751496D7CB","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.6","subdomain":"Security automation","topic":"AI-assisted security documentation","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How should generative AI be used safely for security documentation?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"AI can accelerate drafts, summaries, comments, runbook text, and evidence organization, but generated documentation must be verified against authoritative configuration, logs, policy, and human expertise before it becomes operational truth."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Protect sensitive inputs, record material AI use where required, review citations and commands, and prevent generated text from silently changing policy or response steps."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: An AI tool drafts an incident runbook containing a nonexistent command. A human reviewer validates each operational step in a test environment before publication."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Fluent text is not evidence of correctness. Do not let AI-generated documentation become an unreviewed source of configuration, legal, or incident-response instructions."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>AI-assisted security documentation</b> with SOAR, Runbook, Playbook, Infrastructure as code security. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> AI can accelerate drafts, summaries, comments, runbook text, and evidence organization, but generated documentation must be verified against authoritative configuration, logs, p..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Review an AI-generated security procedure and identify what must be validated before the procedure can be approved."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master AI-assisted security documentation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-AIRMF: NIST AI Risk Management Framework — https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-AIRMF"],"tags":["objective_3.6","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master AI-assisted security documentation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.6-015","objective":"3.6","topic":"AI documentation"}]},{"id":"SX-1146","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-539F79DA3D","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.8","subdomain":"Cryptographic use cases and techniques","topic":"Encrypted tunnel","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What security property does an encrypted tunnel provide?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"An encrypted tunnel protects traffic between tunnel endpoints by applying cryptographic confidentiality and integrity/authentication according to the protocol; it does not automatically secure data before or after those endpoints."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"VPNs, IPsec, TLS-based tunnels, and similar mechanisms protect data in transit across untrusted networks, but endpoint security, routing, authorization, and key management remain separate concerns."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Sensitive traffic crosses the public Internet between two sites. Use an authenticated encrypted tunnel, then still restrict which internal resources the remote site may reach."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A tunnel does not make the entire originating or destination network trusted and cannot protect compromised endpoints from reading plaintext."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Encrypted tunnel</b> with Mutual authentication, VPN, TLS cipher mismatch. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> An encrypted tunnel protects traffic between tunnel endpoints by applying cryptographic confidentiality and integrity/authentication according to the protocol; it does not autom..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given a site-to-site tunnel, identify exactly where plaintext exists and which risks remain outside the tunnel's protection."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Encrypted tunnel when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CRYPTO: NIST Cryptographic Standards and Guidelines — https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CRYPTO"],"tags":["objective_3.8","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Encrypted tunnel when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.8-003","objective":"3.8","topic":"encrypted tunnels"}]},{"id":"SX-1147","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-51BD8D9669","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.8","subdomain":"Cryptographic use cases and techniques","topic":"Non-repudiation","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What does non-repudiation mean in cryptographic use cases?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Non-repudiation is the ability to provide strong evidence linking an action or signed data to a claimed origin so the actor cannot plausibly deny it later, subject to trustworthy identity, key custody, timestamps, and process controls."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Digital signatures can support non-repudiation because a private-key operation can be verified with the corresponding public key, but the surrounding identity proofing and protection of the signing key matter."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A high-value approval must later be attributable to a specific authorized signer. Use a protected signing key, validated certificate/identity, signed transaction, trusted timestamp/audit trail, and controlled signing workflow."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A digital signature proves possession/use of a private key, not automatically the human intent behind every signature; compromised or shared keys undermine non-repudiation claims."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Non-repudiation</b> with Digital signature, Code signing, Centralized key management. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Non-repudiation is the ability to provide strong evidence linking an action or signed data to a claimed origin so the actor cannot plausibly deny it later, subject to trustworth..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Explain why a valid signature from a shared private key provides weaker non-repudiation evidence than one from a personally controlled hardware-backed key."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Non-repudiation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CRYPTO: NIST Cryptographic Standards and Guidelines — https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines; SRC-NIST-NONREP: NIST CSRC Glossary — Non-repudiation — https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/non_repudiation"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0397"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CRYPTO","SRC-NIST-NONREP"],"tags":["objective_3.8","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Non-repudiation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.8-007","objective":"3.8","topic":"non-repudiation"}]},{"id":"SX-1148","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-19BF88A413","domain":"Security Engineering","objective":"3.8","subdomain":"Cryptographic use cases and techniques","topic":"Centralized key management","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What is centralized cryptographic key management?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Centralized key management uses a controlled KMS/HSM or equivalent authority to govern key generation, storage, access, distribution, rotation, revocation, backup/recovery, and auditing under consistent policy."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Centralization can improve oversight and separation of duties but creates a high-value dependency that requires strong availability, access control, logging, and recovery design."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Hundreds of applications generate and store encryption keys independently in configuration files. Move key lifecycle operations to an access-controlled KMS/HSM and give workloads only the minimum key-use permissions they require."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Centralized key management is not the same as storing every plaintext key in one database; keys should remain protected and operations should be mediated by strong cryptographic/key-control systems."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Centralized key management</b> with Symmetric cryptography, Asymmetric cryptography, Digital signature, Hashing. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Centralized key management uses a controlled KMS/HSM or equivalent authority to govern key generation, storage, access, distribution, rotation, revocation, backup/recovery, and..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Design a centralized key-management architecture for multiple applications and explain how you avoid a single operational point of failure."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Centralized key management when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CRYPTO: NIST Cryptographic Standards and Guidelines — https://csrc.nist.gov/projects/cryptographic-standards-and-guidelines"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CRYPTO"],"tags":["objective_3.8","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Centralized key management when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-3.8-017","objective":"3.8","topic":"centralized key management"}]},{"id":"SX-1149","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-C69A5458D5","domain":"Security Operations","objective":"4.1","subdomain":"Monitoring and response data analysis","topic":"Security trend analysis","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What is the purpose of trend analysis in security monitoring and response?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Trend analysis examines security measurements over time to identify persistent change, deterioration, improvement, seasonality, or emerging risk that a single event or snapshot can miss."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Useful trends can include detection volume/quality, patch latency, attack techniques, authentication anomalies, asset exposure, incident causes, or control failures; normalize for environment changes before drawing conclusions."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Phishing alert counts doubled after the company doubled its workforce. Normalize by user/message volume before concluding the phishing rate actually increased."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A trend is not automatically causal, and raw counts can mislead when collection, asset population, or detection logic changes."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Security trend analysis</b> with Security metric, Behavior baseline, Visualization/dashboard. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Trend analysis examines security measurements over time to identify persistent change, deterioration, improvement, seasonality, or emerging risk that a single event or snapshot..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given six months of alert data and a major sensor rollout mid-period, explain how you would decide whether the threat level truly increased."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Security trend analysis when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-OT: NIST SP 800-82 Rev. 3 — Guide to Operational Technology (OT) Security — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/82/r3/final; SRC-CISA-ICS: CISA Industrial Control Systems — https://www.cisa.gov/topics/industrial-control-systems"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0417"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-OT","SRC-CISA-ICS"],"tags":["objective_4.1","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Security trend analysis when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-4.1-009","objective":"4.1","topic":"trends"}]},{"id":"SX-1150","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-125B84FD96","domain":"Security Operations","objective":"4.2","subdomain":"Vulnerabilities, attacks, and mitigations","topic":"Software patching","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What does secure software patching require beyond installing an update?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Software patching identifies applicable fixes, prioritizes them by exposure and risk, tests compatibility, deploys through controlled stages, verifies success, and uses rollback or compensating controls when needed."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Patch urgency should consider active exploitation, asset criticality, vulnerability severity, exposure, business impact, and dependencies; update the source image/package so rebuilt systems do not reintroduce the flaw."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A critical library has an actively exploited vulnerability. Patch a representative staging group, monitor, deploy in phases, verify versions and application health, and update deployment artifacts."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Patching is not complete when a ticket says 'deployed'; failed installations, stale images, dependencies, or unsupported software can leave exposure."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Software patching</b> with Outdated or unpatched software, Automated patching, Dependency management, End-of-life risk. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Software patching identifies applicable fixes, prioritizes them by exposure and risk, tests compatibility, deploys through controlled stages, verifies success, and uses rollback..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Prioritize patches for three systems with different exposure and criticality and justify the order without relying on CVSS alone."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Software patching when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_4.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Software patching when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-4.2-029","objective":"4.2","topic":"software patching"}]},{"id":"SX-1151","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-881E4956E1","domain":"Security Operations","objective":"4.2","subdomain":"Vulnerabilities, attacks, and mitigations","topic":"Least privilege","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What does least privilege require?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Least privilege grants each user, process, device, or service only the permissions and access needed for its authorized task, for only as long as needed."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Implement it through scoped roles/attributes, just-in-time elevation, restricted service accounts/tokens, separation of duties, review of unused rights, and removal of standing administrative access."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A serverless function needs read access to one storage prefix but has administrator access to the entire account. Replace the broad role with a narrowly scoped resource/action policy."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Least privilege is about authorization scope; it is different from least functionality, which minimizes enabled services/features."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Least privilege</b> with Injection, SSRF, TOCTOU, Least functionality. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Least privilege grants each user, process, device, or service only the permissions and access needed for its authorized task, for only as long as needed."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given an overprivileged API token, rewrite its permissions to the minimum set and identify one legitimate operation that would intentionally stop working."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Least privilege when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-LEAST-PRIV: NIST CSRC Glossary — Least Privilege — https://csrc.nist.gov/glossary/term/least_privilege"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0449"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-LEAST-PRIV"],"tags":["objective_4.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Least privilege when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-4.2-033","objective":"4.2","topic":"least privilege"}]},{"id":"SX-1152","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-9BE49FFDF5","domain":"Security Operations","objective":"4.4","subdomain":"Incident-response data and artifacts","topic":"Malware variant matching","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"What is malware variant matching?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Variant matching determines whether a suspicious sample is related to known malware by comparing code, structure, strings, behavior, configuration, metadata, and other stable characteristics despite superficial changes."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Analysts may use fuzzy similarity, code/byte patterns, shared functions, YARA-like signatures, behavior, packer/config artifacts, and lineage clues; a match supports family clustering but not necessarily actor attribution."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: Two binaries have different hashes because one was repacked, but they share distinctive functions, configuration format, C2 behavior, and code similarity. Treat them as likely variants and continue validation."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Hash mismatch does not prove unrelated malware, and code similarity alone does not prove the same threat actor because code can be reused or copied."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Malware variant matching</b> with Malware detonation, Volatile-memory analysis, Network forensics, Timeline reconstruction. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Variant matching determines whether a suspicious sample is related to known malware by comparing code, structure, strings, behavior, configuration, metadata, and other stable ch..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Compare two hypothetical samples and identify which features are strong versus weak evidence that they belong to the same malware family."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Malware variant matching when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-MALWARE: NIST SP 800-83 Rev. 1 — Guide to Malware Incident Prevention and Handling — https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/83/r1/final; SRC-YARA: YARA Documentation — https://virustotal.github.io/yara/"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0485"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-MALWARE","SRC-YARA"],"tags":["objective_4.4","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Malware variant matching when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-4.4-005","objective":"4.4","topic":"variant matching"}]},{"id":"SX-1153","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-ADF0757855","domain":"Security Operations","objective":"4.2","subdomain":"Vulnerabilities, attacks, and mitigations","topic":"Secrets management as a mitigation","card_type":"quality_audit_v4","front":"How does secrets management mitigate application and system vulnerabilities?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Secrets management keeps credentials, tokens, certificates, and keys out of source code and unmanaged configuration by storing them in controlled systems with scoped access, auditing, rotation, and revocation."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Use centralized secret stores or platform-native secret services, short-lived credentials where feasible, automated rotation, secret scanning, and workload identity so applications retrieve only the secrets they need at runtime."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"Scenario: A repository contains a production API key. Revoke and rotate the exposed key, remove it from code/history where appropriate, move future retrieval to a secrets manager, and scope the application identity to only the required secret."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Deleting a secret from the latest commit does not invalidate copies already cloned or logged; exposure requires rotation/revocation. A secrets manager also does not fix overbroad permissions by itself."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Compare <b>Secrets management as a mitigation</b> with Embedded secret, Secrets management, Secret rotation. For each, identify the protected asset, security property, enforcement layer, and scenario cue that would make one choice better than the others.<br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Secrets management keeps credentials, tokens, certificates, and keys out of source code and unmanaged configuration by storing them in controlled systems with scoped access, aud..."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given an application with three hard-coded credentials, design a migration to managed secrets and explain the rotation/revocation sequence."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master Secrets management as a mitigation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-OWASP-SECRETS: OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet — https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Secrets_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":75,"prerequisites":["SX-0451"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-OWASP-SECRETS"],"tags":["objective_4.2","quality_audit_v4","blueprint_coverage"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master Secrets management as a mitigation when you can explain it without notes, apply it to a new SecurityX scenario, contrast the nearest confusable concept, and state a limitation or failure mode.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first. Then justify it with mechanism, scenario evidence, the reason the nearest alternative is worse, and one limitation or tradeoff.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-4.2-035","objective":"4.2","topic":"secrets management"}]},{"id":"SX-1154","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-AUDIT-ASSESS","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.3","subdomain":"Control-effectiveness assessment","topic":"assessments","card_type":"quality_audit_v4_1","front":"CAS-005 objective 2.3 — assessments: what must you know and apply?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"A control assessment uses evidence and testing to determine whether a control is designed, implemented, and operating as intended—not merely documented."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Assessments may combine interviews, configuration review, observation, sampling, technical testing, and exercises. Scope and evidence quality determine how much confidence the result deserves."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"A control is marked “implemented,” but a red-team exercise repeatedly bypasses it. The assessment should record the observed failure, affected risk, evidence, and required corrective action rather than passing the control because documentation exists."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"An assessment is broader than a vulnerability scan. A scan can supply evidence, but it does not by itself establish governance ownership, process performance, or every control objective."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Assessment is one evidence source for <b>control effectiveness</b>; scanning and metrics are complementary evidence. <br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Assess the control in operation, not on paper."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Given an MFA control with policy, technical configuration, and exception records, identify three pieces of evidence you would collect to assess whether the control is actually effective."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master control-effectiveness assessments when you can choose suitable evidence, distinguish design/implementation/operation, and explain why documentation alone is insufficient."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":90,"prerequisites":["SX-0171"],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_2.3","control-effectiveness","quality-audit-v4.1"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master control-effectiveness assessments when you can choose suitable evidence, distinguish design/implementation/operation, and explain why documentation alone is insufficient.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first; then justify it using evidence quality, scenario context, the nearest confusable concept, and one limitation.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.3-018","objective":"2.3","topic":"assessments","card_id":"SX-1154"}]},{"id":"SX-1155","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-AUDIT-SCAN","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.3","subdomain":"Scanning for control effectiveness","topic":"scanning","card_type":"quality_audit_v4_1","front":"CAS-005 objective 2.3 — scanning: what must you know and apply?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Scanning is an automated evidence-gathering technique that can reveal vulnerabilities, exposures, or configuration weaknesses relevant to control effectiveness."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Authenticated and unauthenticated scanners can test different views of an environment. Results must be validated, scoped, prioritized, and interpreted in business context; a scanner finding is evidence, not an automatic risk decision."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"A hardening standard requires unused services to be disabled. An authenticated configuration scan shows 18 servers still expose the service; manual validation confirms the finding. The scan supplies measurable evidence that the hardening control is not fully effective."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"Scanning is not equivalent to penetration testing, a complete assessment, or vulnerability management. It can miss logic flaws, compensate poorly for bad credentials/scope, and generate false positives or false negatives."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Scanning feeds <b>vulnerability management</b> and <b>control assessments</b>; metrics can trend scan results over time. <br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Scan = automated evidence, not final judgment."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"A scanner reports a critical issue on a segmented legacy appliance. Explain what you would validate before declaring the compensating-control design ineffective."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master scanning for control effectiveness when you can state what scans can prove, what they cannot prove, and how to validate and contextualize their output."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":90,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_2.3","control-effectiveness","quality-audit-v4.1"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master scanning for control effectiveness when you can state what scans can prove, what they cannot prove, and how to validate and contextualize their output.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first; then justify it using evidence quality, scenario context, the nearest confusable concept, and one limitation.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.3-019","objective":"2.3","topic":"scanning","card_id":"SX-1155"}]},{"id":"SX-1156","deck":"SecurityX CAS-005 Layered Master Deck","concept_id":"SXC-V4-AUDIT-METRICS","domain":"Security Architecture","objective":"2.3","subdomain":"Control-effectiveness metrics","topic":"metrics","card_type":"quality_audit_v4_1","front":"CAS-005 objective 2.3 — metrics: what must you know and apply?","pages":[{"title":"1 · Direct Answer","content":"Control-effectiveness metrics are measurable indicators tied to a control objective or risk outcome and used to determine whether performance is improving, degrading, or meeting an agreed target."},{"title":"2 · Concept Expansion","content":"Useful metrics have a defined owner, data source, calculation, threshold/target, reporting cadence, and decision purpose. Prefer measures tied to outcomes—such as coverage, failure rate, detection/response time, or exception age—over vanity activity counts."},{"title":"3 · Worked / SecurityX Scenario","content":"A patch program reports “10,000 patches installed,” but critical Internet-facing vulnerabilities remain open past SLA. A better effectiveness metric is the percentage of critical exposed assets remediated within the required time, plus validated exception age."},{"title":"4 · Boundaries and Misconceptions","content":"A metric is not automatically a KPI/KRI and high activity does not imply low risk. Poorly chosen metrics can be gamed, hide denominator problems, or reward volume instead of security outcomes."},{"title":"5 · Connections and Memory","content":"<b>Connections.</b> Metrics summarize evidence from assessments, scans, incidents, and operations. <br><br><b>Memory cue.</b> Measure the security outcome, not just the activity."},{"title":"6 · Transfer Prompt","content":"Design one effectiveness metric for privileged-access management and state its numerator, denominator, target, and what decision it should trigger."},{"title":"7 · Mastery Evidence","content":"Master control-effectiveness metrics when you can design an outcome-linked measure, identify a misleading vanity metric, and explain how the metric drives a decision."},{"title":"8 · Sources","content":"SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005: CompTIA SecurityX CAS-005 Exam Objectives v3.0 — https://comptiacdn.azureedge.net/webcontent/docs/default-source/exam-objectives/comptia-securityx-cas-005-exam-objectives-%283-0%291d9e61d00bce410d87e3bca2ce40fa8a.pdf?sfvrsn=aa502057_0; SRC-NIST-CSF2: NIST Cybersecurity Framework 2.0 — https://www.nist.gov/cyberframework"}],"difficulty":3,"stage":"Intermediate","estimated_seconds":90,"prerequisites":[],"source_ids":["SRC-COMPTIA-CAS005","SRC-NIST-CSF2"],"tags":["objective_2.3","control-effectiveness","quality-audit-v4.1"],"modalities":["verbal","written","scenario"],"mastery_evidence":"Master control-effectiveness metrics when you can design an outcome-linked measure, identify a misleading vanity metric, and explain how the metric drives a decision.","answer_rubric":"Give the direct answer first; then justify it using evidence quality, scenario context, the nearest confusable concept, and one limitation.","blueprint_topics":[{"id":"BP-2.3-020","objective":"2.3","topic":"metrics","card_id":"SX-1156"}]}]);
